PS5 HDMI: SCDC TMDS_Config at every setup for SCDC sinks, as a PC does

The "Beyond TV" (issues #11, #21, ARG-BerserkeR) announces its HDMI 2.1 EDID at boot (FRL rate
6, 120 Hz, 600 MHz, VRR range), then, about 5 s after the console set it up at 1080p60,
re-announces an HDMI 1.4 EDID (max 60 Hz, 340 MHz, no FRL, no VRR): only 1080p60 and 1440p60
are left. At 1080p60 the setup wrote the SCDC Source Version but never TMDS_Config; a PC's
driver writes TMDS_Config at every enable for a sink with SCDC, 0x00 when it doesn't scramble.
On v1.2.0, which started at 4K60 (TMDS_Config written), this TV kept its HDMI 2.1 EDID.
---

--- a/drivers/ps5/hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/ps5/hdmi.c
@@ -1249,14 +1249,20 @@
 		i2c_end_block();
 		i2c_waitclear(0x7a84, 0x01);
 	}
+	/*
+	 * SCDC TMDS_Config: scrambling + 1/40 clock ratio above 340 MHz. written at every
+	 * setup for a sink that talks SCDC, 0x00 at low rates too, as a PC's driver does. The
+	 * "Beyond TV" (issues #11, #21) re-announced an HDMI 1.4 EDID (max 60 Hz, 340 MHz, no 4K120,
+	 * no VRR) a few seconds after a 1080p60 start, where TMDS_Config was never written; it
+	 * kept its HDMI 2.1 EDID when the console started at 4K60, where it was.
+	 */
 	i2c_begin_block();
-	if (vic == 97 || hdmi_mode_is_hi_rate(mode) || scrambling) {
-		/* SCDC TMDS_Config: scrambling + 1/40 clock ratio above 340 MHz */
+	if (vic == 97 || hdmi_mode_is_hi_rate(mode) || scrambling || sink_scdc) {
 		i2c_write(0x7c00, (vic == 97 || hdmi_mode_is_hi_rate(mode)) ? 0x03 : 0x00);
 		i2c_write_data(0x7a80, (u8[]){0xa8, 0x20, 0x00, 0x80}, 4);
 	}
 	i2c_end_block();
-	if (vic == 97 || hdmi_mode_is_hi_rate(mode) || scrambling) {
+	if (vic == 97 || hdmi_mode_is_hi_rate(mode) || scrambling || sink_scdc) {
 		i2c_waitclear(0x7a84, 0x01);
 	}
 	scrambling = vic == 97 || hdmi_mode_is_hi_rate(mode);
